<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">worldmap</ansi>

In the web client, the Map window draws the rooms you have seen as terrain
tiles and shows you on them. The text clients have <ansi fg="command">map</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help map</ansi>) instead.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Terrain</ansi>

  Each room is a small picture of its ground: grass, forest, road, water,
  shore, swamp, snow, desert, cave, dungeon, city and the rest. Rooms that
  touch share an edge, so a region reads as one stretch of country. Water,
  shore, swamp, snow and desert move gently; a setting for reduced motion
  in your browser keeps them still.

  Where two rooms touch but have <ansi fg="yellow">no exit between them</ansi>, a dark edge marks the wall,
  so the map never suggests a way through. An exit between rooms that do
  not touch is a line. A locked door shows a key and a secret passage you
  have found shows a question mark. Stairs up and down show a small
  chevron in the tile's corner.

  An exit you have not been through ends in a patch of <ansi fg="yellow">fog</ansi>: the edge of what
  you know. A room whose ground has no picture shows plain stone.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Landmarks</ansi>

  An inn, bank, trainer, temple, gate, wall, bridge, keep and other
  places drawn from the room's type show a small building or sign over the
  terrain instead of a letter. A room with a symbol of its own but no
  picture keeps its letter, outlined so you can read it. Hover over any
  room for its name and type.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">You</ansi>

  You are drawn as your class: a warrior, cleric, rogue, ranger, wizard or
  witch. Before you have chosen one, or when a class has no picture of its
  own, you are an adventurer. A gold ring under your feet marks your tile,
  so you never blend into the ground.

  When you move, you walk to the next tile and face the way you went:
  north shows your back, south your front, east and west your side. A
  recall or a teleport jumps without walking. Moving up or down a level
  fades you in on the new level.

  Zooming moves between sizes that keep the pixels sharp, so the picture
  never smears.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your company</ansi>

  A small shield with a number sits over you: how many of your company are
  with you (you, and each companion who is present and not separated or
  fallen). It is hidden when you travel alone (<ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>).

  Companions who are with you stand beside you, drawn as their class
  (small, at three quarters of your size) and walking with you. Up to four
  are drawn; the shield still counts everyone. One whose class is not
  known is counted but not drawn.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your party</ansi>

  A party member whose class is known is drawn as that class, a little
  smaller than you and with a small pennant over them. One whose class is
  not known stays a heart. Nobody outside your party appears on the map.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Camps</ansi>

  Your camp shows as a tent on its tile (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>). A lit fire flickers beside
  it with smoke rising; an unlit one sits dark. While your company rests,
  a small sleeping mark floats over the tent; resting at an inn shows the
  inn's bed instead. A fire that has burned down to embers glows low with
  no smoke, and a camp made without a tent shows two bedrolls round a ring
  of stones instead of the tent (<ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>). The camps of your own party show as blue tents, with
  their own fires. You never see the camp of a company that is not in
  your party, even one a step away.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Resources</ansi>

  A small icon in a tile's corner marks what it provides, such as fresh
  water, forage or a fishing spot (<ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>). Up to three show, with a plus
  for more. A resource that has been picked clean has an empty-basket mark
  over its icon, and the mark clears on its own when the resource regrows,
  even in a room you are not standing in. On the Classic style, and on a
  tile zoomed too small for icons, each shows as a coloured dot instead.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Walking by clicking</ansi>

  Click another room and pick <ansi fg="yellow">Walk to [room]</ansi> to have your company walk there
  (<ansi fg="command">help walkto</ansi>). Small dots mark the rooms ahead and a flag marks the
  destination until you arrive or stop; the same click offers <ansi fg="yellow">Stop walking</ansi>
  while a walk is under way. The server only walks to rooms you have visited.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Day and night</ansi>

  Outdoor tiles darken and cool as the game's evening comes, with an hour of
  dusk before nightfall and an hour of dawn after sunrise. Rooms under a roof or
  underground are not shaded, as the sky does not reach them.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Settings</ansi>

  The settings button on the map has <ansi fg="yellow">Style</ansi> (Tiles, or Classic for the plain
  coloured squares and letters, where shape, size and spacing apply),
  <ansi fg="yellow">Sprites</ansi> (on, or off for the classic red square and hearts) and
  <ansi fg="yellow">Camps</ansi> (on, or off to hide every camp marker) and <ansi fg="yellow">Day/night</ansi> (on, or off for no shading). All are kept per browser. The map shows only what the game already tells
  you: nothing about anyone outside your party.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help walkto</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help resources</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help party</ansi>.
